+      "mark": "On the machine and Node version that wrote them the snapshot hash is identical and every summary is identical (relative tolerance 0). A number outside its stated tolerance fails the test; the file is regenerated only by npm run golden:update, and the commit that changes a golden value says why.",
+      "toleranceSameEngine": 0
+    },
+    "crossEngine": {
+      "claim": "The same scenario gives the same snapshot in Node and in a browser worker.",
+      "setup": "The golden scenarios built in Node, handed to the engine in a Web Worker of the production build in Chromium (the app's own SimClient), run to the golden marks.",
+      "mark": "The snapshot hash is identical at every mark. If it is not, the e2e fails, says by how much (which parts of the snapshot differ and the largest difference in the arrival-time raster, in seconds, and in the burnt area), and the goldens get a stated cross-engine tolerance from that measurement."
+    },
+    "tiers": {
+      "claim": "The Fast, Standard and High tiers give about the same fire on real terrain.",
+      "setup": "Katoomba (3 h, with the south-west change 2 h in) and the Warrumbungles (2 h), 6 km, the V20 preset and ignitions of the desktop study, seed 1, fire cells of 30 m with the Fast, the Standard and the High air, and the High tier as the app builds it (20 m cells), with embers on and with embers off; one realisation per row.",
+      "mark": "At each hour the burnt area of a 3-D tier is within 35 percent of the Fast tier's, and at the end at least 80 percent of the 60 m blocks that either run burnt are burnt in both.",
