+# How far Fast, Standard and High differ on real terrain with a fire (idea 21 of plan 15d)
+
+**Question.** The three air tiers (Fast: the forecast wind fitted to the terrain; Standard: 3-D air; High: finer 3-D air) change the wind the fire feels. Nothing
+measured how far they change the fire on real terrain. This is that measurement, once, on two bundled sites.
+
+**Status.** Done in 0.10.0. The numbers are in [`summary.json`](summary.json) (`headline` is the one-sentence form the validation card and the claims register
+quote). **One realisation per row; one shared 4 vCPU machine; no phone, no browser; no tier is shown to be closer to a real fire.**
+
+**Source and licence.** No external input. Terrain, vegetation, canopy and fire history are the bundled files of `public/demo/` (sources and licences in
+`public/demo/provenance.json`). What is committed is output of the repository's own simulation.
+
+## Method
+
+- **Harness.** The one of [`studies/desktop-simulation/01-cost-vs-detail/scripts`](../../desktop-simulation/01-cost-vs-detail/README.md) (its method page), unchanged: `run-batch.mjs`
+  runs each row in its own Node process under the shared heavy lock, `launch.mjs` loads the repository's own TypeScript through Vite, `row.ts` runs the real `Simulation`
+  and keeps burnt area and perimeter every 600 s and a 60 m coarse raster of the final arrival times. Only [`make-specs.mjs`](make-specs.mjs) (the rows) and
+  [`summarize.mjs`](summarize.mjs) (the comparison) are new.
+- **Scenario.** The desktop study's: the V20 preset `hot-nw-sw-change` from 14:00 on 2025-12-20, one 60 m point ignition, seed 1, a 6 km square. Katoomba from
+  (-2170, 900) m for 3 h (the south-west change is two hours in); the Warrumbungles from (-1500, 1000) m for 2 h (the fire fills the square soon after).
+- **Rows** (per site, with embers on and with embers off): **F30** Fast at 30 m cells (the reference), **S30** Standard at 30 m, **H30** the High tier's finer air (a
+  100 m air grid of 24 levels) on the same 30 m fire grid, **F20** Fast at 20 m cells, **H20** the High tier as the app builds it (20 m fire cells with the finer air).
+  F30 against S30 and H30 changes only the air; F30 against F20 changes only the cell; F20 against H20 changes only the air at 20 m.
+- **Embers.** On, the random draws change with every setting, so a difference mixes the air, the cell and chance (the desktop study measured the ember cap alone moving
+  the area by tens of percent). Off, the rows differ only in the air and the cell. Both are reported.
+- **Measures.** The burnt area at each hour; the share of the 60 m blocks that either run burnt which are burnt in both (a block is burnt when at least half its cells are,
+  as in the desktop study's `convergence-diff.json`); the median and 90th percentile of the difference in mean arrival time over the blocks burnt in both.
+- **The pass mark** was written before the run, in `src/sim/validation/passMarks.json` (`tiers`): at every hour the burnt area of a 3-D tier within 35 percent of the Fast
+  tier's (the literature's adequacy interval quoted in `../README.md`), and at least 80 percent of the 60 m blocks burnt in both at the end (that 0.8 is a choice, not from a
+  source). The verdict per comparison is in `summary.json` (`verdicts`).
+
+## Reproduce
+
+From the repository root, on a machine with its `node_modules`:
+
+```
+node studies/simulation-validity/tiers/make-specs.mjs > specs.json
+setsid nohup node studies/desktop-simulation/01-cost-vs-detail/scripts/run-batch.mjs specs.json OUT_DIR OUT_DIR/log.txt &   # 20 rows, each under flock /tmp/firesim-heavy.lock
+node studies/simulation-validity/tiers/summarize.mjs OUT_DIR studies/simulation-validity/tiers/summary.json $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)
+node scripts/validity-card.mjs      # remakes the card's data and the claims page from it
+```
+
+## Limits
+
+- One realisation per row; embers-on rows especially are single draws. No repeat to measure the noise of an ember run (the desktop study saw 1 to 3 percent in wall time, and
+  tens of percent in area between ember caps).
+- Two sites, one weather scenario (a hot, windy day with a change), one ignition each. Katoomba is NSW sandstone country with steep escarpments; the Warrumbungles are grass woodland.
+- A difference between tiers is a difference between model settings. Agreement would not be accuracy either: the tiers share the same spread formulas.
+- The timing in the rows is not a result of this study: the machine was shared with other jobs (several lanes were running at once) and the figures vary with that load.
