+# Validity checks (release 0.10.0, plan 15d)
+
+What the simulation is checked against, how to run the checks, and what they found. The question behind them: **how far can a number the app shows be trusted?**
+The answer so far, in one sentence: it is held to its own formulas, to published rules of thumb, to properties that must hold and to a finer run of itself,
+and **not to a recorded fire, another fire model or measured wind over hills**.
+
+The register of every claim is [`claims.md`](claims.md) (made from `src/sim/validation/claims.json`). The desk research and ideas are in
+[`studies/simulation-validity/`](../../studies/simulation-validity/README.md).
+
+## What is checked, and where
+
+| Check | Files | Asks | Run |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| V1 to V22 (before this release) | `src/sim/validation/*.test.ts`, `src/sim/validation.test.ts` | Does the code do what the specification and published rules of thumb say? | `npx vitest run src/sim/validation/` (SLOW=1 for the slow ones) |
+| **Properties** | `src/sim/validation/properties.test.ts`, `randomCases.ts`, `propertyKit.ts` | Turn the map, add wind, add moisture, add a firebreak, make a change later: must the result move the way it should? Over seeded random terrains, no reference answer. | `SLOW=1 npx vitest run src/sim/validation/properties.test.ts` |
+| **Convergence** | `src/sim/validation/convergence.test.ts` | Does a smaller step or finer cells give nearly the same fire, inside a band written first? Synthetic terrain, Katoomba, Warrumbungles. | `SLOW=1 npx vitest run src/sim/validation/convergence.test.ts` |
+| **Stress and fuzz** | `src/sim/validation/fuzz.test.ts`, `fuzzCases.ts` | Random terrain, weather and ignitions, extremes included: any NaN, impossible area, runaway growth, or a burnt cell that un-burns? | `SLOW=1 npx vitest run src/sim/validation/fuzz.test.ts`; a long sweep: `FUZZ_FROM=3001 FUZZ_N=500 FUZZ_STD_N=20 SLOW=1 npx vitest run src/sim/validation/fuzz.test.ts` |
+| **Golden scenarios** | `src/sim/validation/golden/` | Six fixed scenarios whose summaries and snapshot hash are stored: is a change in results noticed? | `npx vitest run src/sim/validation/golden/golden.test.ts`; rewrite with `npm run golden:update` (prints the diff; refuses to run when `CI` is set) |
+| **Across engines** | `e2e/validity-engines.spec.ts`, `scripts/validity-engines-export.mjs` | Does the same scenario give the same snapshot in Node and in a Chromium Web Worker? | `bash <lane helper> <worktree> <port> e2e/validity-engines.spec.ts` (builds, serves, runs Playwright) |
+| **Tiers** | `studies/simulation-validity/tiers/` | How far do Fast, Standard and High differ on real terrain with a fire? | see its README (20 rows, about half an hour) |
+| **The register and the card** | `src/sim/validation/claims.json`, `claims.test.ts`, `claimsKit.ts`, `scripts/validity-card.mjs`, `src/ui/validityCard.ts` | Does every claim name a check that exists? Is the card made from the register and the run, with no figure typed by hand? | `node scripts/validity-card.mjs` remakes `card.json` and `claims.md` |
+
+## The pass marks were written first
+
+`src/sim/validation/passMarks.json` was committed **before** any property, golden, fuzz, tier or cross-engine code existed (its first commit contains nothing else). Each check
+reads its number from it, so the mark tested is the mark written first. Where a mark could not be met, the file's `revisions` says what changed and why, and tells apart
+a mark **set after measuring**, a **narrowing** and a **failed mark reported as a finding**. Nothing was widened to make a check pass.
+
+## What the checks found (reported, not fixed: the solver was not touched)
+
+- **Rotation is exact to rounding, not bit for bit.** A terrain, wind and ignition turned by 90, 180 or 270 degrees give the turned fire; the burnt cells at every 600 s mark were equal in
+  all 24 turned runs but the arrival times differed by up to 0.0017 s (a few units of a 32-bit float; floating-point order). The pre-registered bit for bit mark was missed; the check asserts the
+  measured 0.01 s.
+- **F1: the Head fire figure can be above the model's speed limit.** `SimStats.headRos`, which the Head fire tile shows, reads the stored rate of the freshest front cell, and in the probes the cell holding the large value was an ignition
+  cell, whose stored rate is the model's own and not held to the 6 m/s (21.6 km/h) cap (the cause is read from the probes, not proven). A line ignition on steep ground shows far more for the first minutes (fuzz seed 1027: 28.2 m/s, about 101 km/h),
+  and the value is kept for minutes, also after the front has died out (seed 1030, constant 12 percent moisture: 6.14 m/s from 60 s to 600 s through minutes with no cell burning, then 37.6 m/s at 900 s with three cells burning after a later line ignition; seed 1037: 26.2 m/s held for ten minutes while the fire grew). The front itself never outruns the cap. Pinned by `F-fuzz-F1`, which fails the day it is fixed.
+  How often: in 3 of the first 40 fast-tier seeds, and in the long sweep (`src/sim/validation/results/fuzz-sweep.json`, 500 fast-tier and 20 standard-tier cases) in 22 and 1 of them; no other invariant broke in any of the 560 distinct cases.
+  To reproduce: `drawFuzz(1027)` in `src/sim/validation/fuzzCases.ts`, run it, read `stats.headRos` of the first snapshots.
+- **F2: one solver step case is just outside its band.** On the synthetic terrain of seed 601, a 2 s step gives 130.3 ha at 1 h against 118.1 ha for the automatic step: 10.4 percent, against a band of 10.
+  Everything else is inside (the 5 s step within 3.2 percent everywhere; the 2 s step within 7.3 percent on both bundled sites and three other seeds). Pinned by a `.fails` test.
+- **An observation, not a defect:** `SimStats.deadFuelMoistureMean` is NaN when the fire has burnt every burnable cell (seed 1002 burnt its whole square); the UI shows a dash.
+  The fuzz allows it only then.
+- **Everything else in the run held:** the other properties, the grid bands on every case, the golden summaries, the stress invariants, and (see `src/sim/validation/results/cross-engine.json` and
+  the card) the cross-engine check. The figures are in `src/sim/validation/results/validity-run.json`.
+- **What no check can say:** that any of it matches a real fire. Finer cells and the 3-D tiers are different settings of the same model; nothing here shows which is closer. At Katoomba 30 m against
+  10 m differs by tens of percent over 4 h (the desktop study's data, `studies/desktop-simulation/01-cost-vs-detail/data/convergence-diff.json`).
+
+## Not built, and why
+
+- A grass-fire rate-of-spread test on the 1986 Annaburroo experiments: the dataset's public record was reached once (CC BY 4.0, public, two files); the files were not fetched. See
+  `studies/simulation-validity/annaburroo/README.md`. The orchestrator decides.
+- Hindcasts of recorded fires and a peer comparison (ELMFIRE, FARSITE): one attempt each, both refused (HTTP 403); they need data and a second program the sandbox cannot reach. Recorded as
+  `C-HINDCAST-PEER`.
+- A phone's JavaScript engine: the cross-engine check is Node against Chromium only.
+
+## Docs notes (for the docs lane)
+
+- The model card's *How sure are we?* section gained the validation card: five facts (properties, fixed scenarios, stress cases, same result in a browser, claims in the register), "How far a finer grid
+  changes the answer", a sentence about the tiers, "Not known" and a closing warning. Words: `src/ui/validityCard.ts`; figures: `src/sim/validation/card.json`.
+- New commands: `npm run golden:update`, `node scripts/validity-card.mjs [--merge RUN.jsonl REPORT.json]`, `node scripts/validity-engines-export.mjs <dir>`.
+- The Help and Glossary content was not touched. The register lists two places where a Help figure is not checked against the code: the eruptive slope ("about 24 degrees" against a logistic centred at 22,
+  claim `C-ERUPTIVE`) and the moisture thresholds (6 and 20 percent, `C-MOISTURE-SLOWS`).
